The Security Council,

Reaffirming its relevant resolutions, including resolutions 242 (1967), 338 (1973), 1397 (2002), 1515 (2003), 1850 (2008), 1860 (2009), and 2334 (2016),

Guided by the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations,

Recalling the Universal Declaration of Human Rights and the relevant international human rights treaties, and reaffirming the obligation to respect and ensure respect for international humanitarian law, in particular the Geneva Convention relative to the Protection of Civilian Persons in Time of War, of 12 August 1949,

Expressing grave concern at the escalation of violence and tensions and the deteriorating situation in the Occupied Palestinian Territory, including East Jerusalem, particularly in the Gaza Strip, since 30 March 2018,

Deeply deploring the loss of civilian lives, including among children, women, and humanitarian and medical personnel, and the high number of injuries resulting from the use of force by Israeli forces against Palestinian civilians who are exercising their right to peaceful protest,

Condemning all acts of violence against civilians, including acts of terror, as well as all acts of provocation, incitement, and destruction,

Expressing grave concern also at the dire humanitarian situation and socioeconomic conditions in the Gaza Strip, exacerbated by the prolonged Israeli closure and restrictions on the movement of persons and goods, and emphasizing the need for urgent humanitarian assistance and unhindered access for relief supplies,

Reaffirming that a just, lasting, and comprehensive solution to the Israeli-Palestinian conflict can only be achieved by peaceful means, based on relevant United Nations resolutions, the Madrid terms of reference, including the principle of land for peace, the Arab Peace Initiative, and the Quartet Roadmap, leading to the realization of the two-State solution of two democratic States, Israel and Palestine, living side by side in peace and security within recognized borders,

1. Calls for the consideration of measures to guarantee the safety, protection, and well-being of the Palestinian civilian population in the Occupied Palestinian Territory, including in the Gaza Strip;

2. Deplores the use of any excessive, disproportionate, and indiscriminate force by Israeli forces against Palestinian civilians, including in the Gaza Strip, and in particular the use of live ammunition against peaceful demonstrators, including children, medical personnel, and journalists, and expresses its grave concern at the continued high number of casualties;

3. Demands that Israel, the occupying Power, fully abide by its legal obligations and responsibilities under the Fourth Geneva Convention relative to the Protection of Civilian Persons in Time of War, of 12 August 1949, and refrain from the use of excessive and disproportionate force against peaceful demonstrators;

4. Calls for the taking of urgent steps to ensure an immediate, durable, and fully respected ceasefire, and calls upon all parties to exercise maximum restraint and de-escalate tensions;

5. Calls for immediate and significant steps towards ending the closure and the restrictions imposed by Israel on movement and access into and out of the Gaza Strip, including through the sustained and permanent opening of the crossing points for the flow of humanitarian aid, commercial goods, and persons, in accordance with international humanitarian law and resolution 1860 (2009);

6. Demands that all parties cooperate with international humanitarian relief personnel and facilitate rapid, safe, and unhindered humanitarian access throughout the Occupied Palestinian Territory;

7. Calls for immediate steps to prevent all acts of violence against civilians, including acts of terror, as well as all acts of provocation and destruction, and calls for accountability in this regard;

8. Requests the Secretary-General to examine the present situation and to submit a written report to the Security Council, as soon as possible and no later than 60 days from the adoption of the present resolution, containing his recommendations regarding ways and means for ensuring the safety, protection, and well-being of the Palestinian civilian population under Israeli occupation, including, inter alia, recommendations regarding an international protection mechanism;

9. Decides to remain seized of the matter.

